--TEST--
`Core\Ldap\LdapError` is a class in spec § 10's tree under `RuntimeError`, caught by its own name and its parent's and never by `Core\Db\DbError`, and one a program builds itself has the kind `Protocol` and a `null` code — ADR 0278 § 10
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0278 § 10: one class for every LDAP failure, with the condition as `kind`.
try {
    throw new Core\Ldap\LdapError("the directory is down");
} catch (Core\Ldap\LdapError $down) {
    echo "own: ", $down->message, "\n";
}

// Under `RuntimeError`, beside `Core\Db\DbError` and not below it.
try {
    throw new Core\Ldap\LdapError("the bind was refused");
} catch (Core\Db\DbError $db) {
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "parent: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// No server classified this error, so its kind is `Protocol`, the kind for a
// result code no other kind covers, and there is no result code at all.
Core\Ldap\LdapError $built = new Core\Ldap\LdapError("built by hand");
echo $built->kind == Core\Ldap\ErrorKind::Protocol ? "kind: Protocol" : "kind: other", "\n";
echo $built->code == null ? "code: null" : "code: set", "\n";

// `$kind` is a closed enum, so a `match` names its cases.
echo match ($built->kind) {
    Core\Ldap\ErrorKind::InvalidCredentials => "a wrong login",
    Core\Ldap\ErrorKind::Unavailable => "no server",
    default => "something else",
}, "\n";
--EXPECT--
own: the directory is down
parent: the bind was refused
kind: Protocol
code: null
something else
